{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01L62jRk72PyXLehCU8UkwPC","type":"tool_result","content":"{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"attempted_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"categories_per_panel\": 6,\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 8,\n      \"fallback_reason\": \"generate-concept-figure exited non-zero (rc=1)\",\n      \"figure_id\": \"overview_protocol\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 8,\n        \"legend\": 8,\n        \"ticks\": 8,\n        \"title\": 9\n      },\n      \"generator\": null,\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 24,\n      \"n_panels\": 1,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/overview_protocol.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"prompt_log_path\": \"figures/overview_protocol.prompts.json\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": 1,\n      \"source_ids\": [],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"categories_per_panel\": 4,\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 8,\n      \"figure_id\": \"design_scores\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"bar\",\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 9,\n        \"legend\": 8,\n        \"ticks\": 8,\n        \"title\": 9.5\n      },\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"max_label_chars\": 16,\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/design_scores.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/design_scores.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": 1,\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.score_crowd\",\n        \"final.metric.score_sports\",\n        \"final.metric.score_expert_human\",\n        \"final.metric.score_llm_judge\",\n        \"final.metric.score_constant\",\n        \"final.metric.score_random\",\n        \"final.metric.score_winrate\",\n        \"final.metric.score_influence\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    }\n  ]\n}\ndesign_scores.pdf\ndesign_scores.pdf.prov.json\ndesign_scores.png\ndesign_scores.png.prov.json\noverview_protocol.png\noverview_protocol.prompts.json\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"How Few Edits Displace the Top of a Bradley-Terry Leaderboard? A Verified First-Order Influence Attack Across Crowd, Expert, LLM-Judge and Sports Comparison Data\",\n  \"central_question\": \"How many edits (0.1% to 2% of comparison rows, by dropping or flipping) does it take for a first-order influence search to dethrone the rank-1 competitor of a ridge Bradley-Terry leaderboard, and does that vulnerability differ across evaluation designs (crowd votes, expert human judgments, LLM-judge judgments, sports results)?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"Under evaluator-verified exact refits, a single one-shot first-order influence edit search yields very different rank-1 vulnerability across evaluation designs: it displaces rank 1 in every crowd-vote and sports case at the 2% budget but in none of the LLM-judge cases and only in the flip mode for expert-human judgments; this is a measured, bounded result for one attack and one target (rank 1), not a general sensitivity law, and no improved search method was established.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The only completed, scored evidence is the sealed final evaluation of the frozen selection, which is the original first-order influence starter (selection_kind=baseline, investigation_outcome=completed_no_winner). All three attempted improved search variants were unscored (infrastructure failures), so they are missing evidence, not losses, and cannot support any improvement claim. The final evaluation is qualified/completed and gives (i) a family-averaged edit success of 0.606, (ii) per-design scores (crowd 1.000, sports 0.833, expert-human 0.374, LLM-judge 0.216), (iii) per-design/per-mode top-1 displacement rates at the largest budget, (iv) aggregate evaluator-owned baselines (random 0.071, constant 0.071, win-rate heuristic 0.574), (v) a permuted-input control gap of 0 and a valid proposal rate of 1. This supports a measurement paper whose message is heterogeneity across evaluation designs under a verified attack, with explicit scope limits. A method-improvement narrative would be unsupported; a negative-results framing about the tested method (no improvement over the baseline was established; margin versus the best baseline is 0 because the selected method is the influence baseline) is honest and still informative. The three-way contrast (human crowd vs. MT-Bench human vs. MT-Bench GPT-4 judge vs. NBA) directly answers the task's cross-design question.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Not in edit mode: no prior manuscript exists. A fresh thesis-first framing was chosen over a method-improvement story (rejected because no variant was scored) and over a noise-vs-intent sensitivity story (rejected because the artifacts contain no noise-calibration or subsampling experiments).\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"No staged prior paper or new program inputs; downstream_study shows the frozen selection is byte-identical to the baseline (same program tree hash), so there is a single studied method.\",\n    \"Three attempted variants (re-linearised greedy, pair-class multi-rival greedy, rival-chunked prefix line search) have 0 scored attempts of 3; they appear at most as one sentence in limitations/supplement as unscored design attempts with no performance evidence.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"First-order influence edit search (studied method; frozen selection)\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"It is the only method with scored evidence. One ridge-BT fit, a sensitivity direction for the top-vs-runner-up gap via the penalised-Hessian solve, then rank rows by first-order gap change for drop or flip. Present it as the object of study, not as a novel contribution.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Evaluator-owned reference edit policies: constant, random, win-rate heuristic\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Give scale to the scores: constant and random sit near 0.071, win-rate near 0.574 (family-averaged). Only aggregate values are available; per-family baseline values must not be invented.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Verification controls: exact trusted refit, permuted-input control, valid-proposal rate\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Justify that scores are not self-reported (the evaluator refits the edited data) and that results are not an artifact of row order or identity (permuted-input gap 0; valid proposal rate 1.0).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Unscored improved-search variants\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"Optional one-paragraph disclosure only (3 attempted, 0 scored); no comparison is drawn.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Per-design edit success of the influence search (crowd 1.000, sports 0.833, expert-human 0.374, LLM-judge 0.216; equally weighted mean 0.606), sealed final scope.\",\n    \"Top-1 displacement rate at the largest (2%) budget by design and64:\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/overview_protocol.png}\n91:Choosing a small set of training points to manipulate so as to change a fitted model is the subject of data-poisoning research, in which an attacker alters a fraction of the training data \\citep{Seetharaman2021Influence}. For preference data, \\citet{Yang2026Preference} study label-flip attacks on log-linear direct preference optimisation, observing that flipping one preference label shifts the gradient independently of the parameters, which turns the choice of flips into a sparse-approximation problem. Our reversal mode is a label-flip edit and our drop mode a deletion, and the first-order scores in Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} are likewise additive per row. The setting differs: the model is a Bradley--Terry leaderboard rather than a preference-optimised language model, the objective is to dislodge one rank rather than to steer parameters towards a target, and an evaluator refits the exact model for verification. We cite this literature for positioning only and do not compare against any poisoning method, and we have not surveyed attacks on rank aggregation exhaustively.\n149:Crowd votes & Held-out partition of public Chatbot Arena human-preference votes \\citep{Chiang2024Chatbot} & 2 (drop, flip) & Many chatbots; the largest dataset \\\\\n150:Expert human & MT-Bench expert human pairwise judgments \\citep{Zheng2023Judging}; ties kept & 2 & Six models \\\\\n151:LLM judge & GPT-4 pairwise judgments on the same MT-Bench comparisons; two tie labels mapped to a tie & 2 & Six models \\\\\n172:\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/design_scores.pdf}\n208:The valid proposal rate is $1.0$: every answer to every budget level passed the format checks. The permuted-input control gap is $0.0$, so on the row-shuffled, relabelled copies the proposer obtained the same scores as on the original order, and the measured scores do not depend on row order or competitor labels. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.valid_proposal_rate]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.perm_control_abs_gap]]\n222:\\item \\emph{Number of competitors and graph density.} MT-Bench data involve six models, whereas the crowd and sports data involve more competitors. Whether the number of competitors helps or hinders displacement is not established by four designs.\n# Agentic evidence audit feedback\n\n- generated_at: `2026-10-28T21:48:22.794928+00:00`\n- ok: `False`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `3`\n- audit_json_path: `work/results/t06/claude/2026102