{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVNbk8QokSmoFeTKU4yP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1687,"cache_read_input_tokens":80028,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1687,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f430a8f7-9222-4a1e-acca-edaad3f1df26","uuid":"e147bde0-58f5-4732-b95e-5c0b0fd1e28a","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:49:49.913Z","request_id":"req_011CgVNbhEY61yz1rsieBCEA"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVNbk8QokSmoFeTKU4yP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013YTcaZWpyBF3Whc7tBH7jb","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; $TMPDIR/pyenv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\np='paper.tex'\ns=open(p).read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(a)==1,a\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\nrep(\"recent generalisations model margins of victory \\\\citep{Szczecinski2020GElo} and use the likelihood score as the update direction \\\\citep{Holy2026ScoreDriven}.\",\n    \"recent work generalises Elo to the margin of victory \\\\citep{Szczecinski2020GElo} and proposes a score-driven rating system for sports \\\\citep{Holy2026ScoreDriven}.\")\nrep(\"Choosing a small set of training points to manipulate so as to change a fitted model is the subject of data-poisoning research, in which an attacker alters a fraction of the training data \\\\citep{Seetharaman2021Influence}. For preference data, \\\\citet{Yang2026Preference} study label-flip attacks on log-linear direct preference optimisation, observing that flipping one preference label shifts the gradient independently of the parameters, which turns the choice of flips into a sparse-approximation problem. Our reversal mode\",\n    \"Choosing a small set of training points to manipulate so as to change a fitted model is the subject of data-poisoning research; influence-based reasoning has also been used on the defensive side, against poisoning in online learning \\\\citep{Seetharaman2021Influence}. For preference data, \\\\citet{Yang2026Preference} present a preference-poisoning attack on offline reinforcement learning from human feedback. We know these two works only from their titles and abstract-level metadata, and describe them no further. Our reversal mode\")\nrep(\"for which \\\\citet{Giordano2018Swiss} give finite-sample error bounds under explicit assumptions and show conditions under which it estimates leave-$k$-out refits consistently for fixed $k$; accuracy is therefore a property of the problem and of the size of the perturbation, not guaranteed.\",\n    \"which \\\\citet{Giordano2018Swiss} develop as a general approximation to refits with data removed; its accuracy is a property of the problem and of the size of the perturbation, and is not guaranteed for the 2\\\\% edits studied here.\")\nrep(\"the finite-sample error bounds for the underlying linear approximation depend on problem-dependent quantities \\\\citep{Giordano2018Swiss}, and we did not evaluate them for these data.\",\n    \"the accuracy of the underlying linear approximation is problem dependent \\\\citep{Giordano2018Swiss}, and we did not evaluate it for these data.\")\nrep(\"Dataset sizes are described qualitatively only; no row counts are used in this paper.\",\"Dataset sizes and competitor counts are not used in this paper.\")\nrep(\"Many chatbots; the largest dataset\",\"Anonymised chatbots\")\nrep(\"& 2 & Six models \\\\\\\\\\nLLM judge\",\"& 2 & Anonymised models \\\\\\\\\\nLLM judge\")\nrep(\"& 2 & Six models \\\\\\\\\\nSports\",\"& 2 & Anonymised models \\\\\\\\\\nSports\")\nrep(\"MT-Bench data involve six models, whereas the crowd and sports data involve more competitors. Whether the number of competitors helps or hinders displacement is not established by four designs.\",\n    \"The designs differ in the number of competitors in ways not measured here. Whether the number of competitors helps or hinders displacement is not established by four designs.\")\nrep(\"The permuted-input control gap is $0.0$, so on the row-shuffled, relabelled copies the proposer obtained the same scores as on the original order, and the measured scores do not depend on row order or competitor labels.\",\n    \"The permuted-input control gap is $0.0$: on the single row-shuffled, relabelled control run per case, the proposer's scores matched its scores on the original order (mean absolute gap $0.0$). This is one permutation per case and does not establish invariance to row order or competitor labels in general.\")\nrep(\"\\\\includegraphics[width=\\\\textwidth]{figures/overview_protocol.png}\",\"\\\\includegraphics[width=0.95\\\\textwidth]{figures/overview_protocol.pdf}\")\nrep(\"Comparison rows from four evaluation designs are fitted with a ridge Bradley--Terry model, which fixes the rank-1 competitor and its gap to the runner-up. A proposer\",\"Comparison rows from four evaluation designs are fitted with a ridge Bradley--Terry model, which fixes the rank-1 competitor and its gap $G$ to the runner-up. A proposer\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"Six models\\|six models\\|largest\\|Anonymised\" paper.tex | head; sed -n 160,190p claims_grounding.json; python3 -c \"\nimport json;d=json.load(open('claims_grounding.json'))\nfor c in d['claims']:\n  if any(k in c['claim'].lower() for k in ['permut','size','six','yang','poison','giordano','elo']): print(json.dumps(c)[:600])\""},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1687,"cache_read_input_tokens":80028,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1687,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f430a8f7-9222-4a1e-acca-edaad3f1df26","uuid":"09d590e7-98ab-47ae-9e4f-ae29bba46401","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:49:58.440Z","request_id":"req_011CgVNbhEY61yz1rsieBCEA"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013YTcaZWpyBF3Whc7tBH7jb","type":"tool_result","content":"130:For each budget level, the evaluator refits the edited data with its own fitter, cross-checked against an independent L-BFGS fit, and ignores any quantity the proposer reports about gaps or ranks. The score of a level is $1$ if the original top competitor is no longer first, and otherwise $\\mathrm{clip}(1-G_{\\text{after}}/G_{\\text{before}},0,1)$, the fraction of the gap that was closed. The \\emph{edit success score} of a case is the mean over the five budgets and over evaluation seeds; the score of a design is the mean over its cases; and the \\emph{family-averaged} edit success is the equally weighted ($0.25$ each) mean over the four designs. The \\emph{top-1 displacement rate at the 2\\% budget} of a design and mode is the mean of the displacement indicator at the largest budget over the cases and seeds in that cell. Invalid or missing answers count as worst case. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#aggregate]]\n149:Crowd votes & Held-out partition of public Chatbot Arena human-preference votes \\citep{Chiang2024Chatbot} & 2 (drop, flip) & Anonymised chatbots \\\\\n150:Expert human & MT-Bench expert human pairwise judgments \\citep{Zheng2023Judging}; ties kept & 2 & Anonymised models \\\\\n151:LLM judge & GPT-4 pairwise judgments on the same MT-Bench comparisons; two tie labels mapped to a tie & 2 & Anonymised models \\\\\n177:\\subsection{Top-1 displacement at the largest budget}\n303:\\item Aggregate rows into a $K\\times K$ weighted win-count matrix (a tie adds $\\tfrac12$ to each direction) and solve Eq.~\\eqref{eq:bt} by Newton iterations (at most 100) until the largest gradient entry falls below $10^{-10}$.\n304:\\item Take $\\mathrm{top}$ and $\\mathrm{rv}$ as the two largest scores, and solve $A v=e_{\\mathrm{top}}-e_{\\mathrm{rv}}$.\n  {\n   \"claim\": \"Related work on infinitesimal-jackknife accuracy, poisoning and label-flip attacks on preference data, and Elo-style sports rating (qualitative positioning).\",\n   \"source_ids\": [\n    \"ref.openalex.W2896388162\",\n    \"ref.openalex.W7160306608\",\n    \"ref.openalex.W4287201019\",\n    \"ref.openalex.W3211957373\",\n    \"ref.openalex.W7153982762\"\n   ],\n   \"citation_keys\": [\n    \"Giordano2018Swiss\",\n    \"Yang2026Preference\",\n    \"Seetharaman2021Influence\",\n    \"Szczecinski2020GElo\",\n    \"Holy2026ScoreDriven\"\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"claim\": \"Related work on influence functions, robustness to dropping data, vote rigging, leaderboard practices and the Arena and MT-Bench data.\",\n   \"source_ids\": [\n    \"ref.starter.Koh2017Understanding\",\n    \"ref.starter.Broderick2020Automatic\",\n    \"ref.starter.Min2025Rigging\",\n    \"ref.starter.Singh2025Illusion\",\n    \"ref.starter.Chiang2024Chatbot\",\n    \"ref.starter.Zheng2023Judging\"\n   ],\n   \"citation_keys\": [\n    \"Koh2017Understanding\",\n    \"Broderick2020Automatic\",\n    \"Min2025Rigging\",\n{\"claim\": \"Valid proposal rate 1.0 and permuted-input control gap 0.0.\", \"source_ids\": [\"final.metric.valid_proposal_rate\", \"final.metric.perm_control_abs_gap\"], \"citation_keys\": []}\n{\"claim\": \"Related work on infinitesimal-jackknife accuracy, poisoning and label-flip attacks on preference data, and Elo-style sports rating (qualitative positioning).\", \"source_ids\": [\"ref.openalex.W2896388162\", \"ref.openalex.W7160306608\", \"ref.openalex.W4287201019\", \"ref.openalex.W3211957373\", \"ref.openalex.W7153982762\"], \"citation_keys\": [\"Giordano2018Swiss\", \"Yang2026Preference\", \"Seetharaman2021Influence\", \"Szczecinski2020GElo\", \"Holy2026ScoreDriven\"]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f430a8f7-9222-4a1e-acca-edaad3f1df26","uuid":"05c925b0-7767-4f48-9843-fcdbafe29307","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:49:58.537Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"130:For each budget level, the evaluator refits th